    "english_commentary": [
      {
        "source": "valmikiramayan.net",
        "net_locator": "1-1-26, 1-1-27",
        "spans_beyond_this_verse": true,
        "text": "Here devamaayaa refers to many concepts. VaiShNavaite tenets tell that the word deva is ascribable only to Vishnu, but not to other gods. Thus, this is ViShNumaayaa, when He assumed an extraordinary female form called Mohini, when distributing amR^ita, the divine elixir, to gods and demons. Next is the form of Tilottama, a divine beauty to hoodwink demons called Sunda and Upasunda. The other is Vishnu's liilaa shakti, which is divinely fascinating. Yet another is grammatical connotation, maa yaa where, maa= Goddess Lakshmi; yaa= who that is; meaning yaa siitaa saa maa= 'who is Goddess Lakshmi... She is Seetha...' and this results in the saying: ati ruupavatii siitaa... ati muurkhaH cha raavaNa 'While Seetha is extraordinarily beautiful, Ravana is exceedingly stupid..."
      }
    ],
